From: morgonf <morgonf@altlinux.org>
Subject: [PATCH] ipa: simple: af: Let the lens settle after every step

The software ISP runs behind the sensor: the statistics that arrive right
after a lens move still come from a frame taken at the old position. The
sweep credited the sharpness to the wrong position, the best position came
out at random (96, 56, 38, 58, 98 on a still scene) and the reference
sharpness was a maximum the lens never had. Focus loss then fired half a
second later and the viewfinder pumped forever.

Skip one statistics period after every step (two after a jump), measure
the reference sharpness again at the chosen position, and need a 50 %
deviation for four statistics to call focus lost. Coarse steps of 10 %
and a fine span of 6 % keep the sweep at about 2.5 s. Each sweep step is
logged at debug level.
---
--- a/src/ipa/simple/algorithms/af.cpp
+++ b/src/ipa/simple/algorithms/af.cpp
@@ -35,16 +35,25 @@
  *
  * A search has two passes: coarse with kCoarseStep over the whole travel,
  * then fine with kFineStep within kFineSpan of the best coarse position.
- * Only frames with fresh statistics count, and one statistics period is
- * skipped after a lens jump. In continuous mode focus is considered lost
- * when the sharpness differs from the focused value by more than kLostRatio
- * for kLostCount statistics in a row.
+ * Only frames with fresh statistics count. The software ISP runs behind
+ * the sensor, so the statistics that arrive right after a lens move still
+ * come from a frame taken at the old position: kSettleStats statistics are
+ * skipped after every step, and one more after a jump. Without this the
+ * sharpness was credited to the wrong position, the best position came out
+ * at random and the focus never settled.
+ *
+ * After a sweep the reference sharpness is measured again at the chosen
+ * position (it is lower than the sweep maximum when the scene is noisy).
+ * In continuous mode focus is considered lost when the sharpness differs
+ * from the reference by more than kLostRatio for kLostCount statistics in
+ * a row.
  */
-static constexpr double kCoarseStep = 8.0;
+static constexpr double kCoarseStep = 10.0;
 static constexpr double kFineStep = 2.0;
-static constexpr double kFineSpan = 8.0;
-static constexpr double kLostRatio = 0.4;
-static constexpr unsigned int kLostCount = 3;
+static constexpr double kFineSpan = 6.0;
+static constexpr unsigned int kSettleStats = 1;
+static constexpr double kLostRatio = 0.5;
+static constexpr unsigned int kLostCount = 4;
 
 /*
  * Statistics used to measure the reference sharpness again after a
@@ -196,7 +205,7 @@
 	f.start = 0;
 	f.stop = 100;
 	f.step = kCoarseStep;
-	f.skip = 1;
+	f.skip = kSettleStats + 1;
 	k.focus_pos = 0;
 	k.focus_sweep = true;
 	k.afState = controls::AfStateScanning;
@@ -253,21 +262,24 @@
 		return;
 	}
 
+	LOG(IPASoftAutoFocus, Debug) << "Sweep pos " << focus_pos << " sharpness " << sharp;
 	if (sharp > f.sharpness_max) {
 		f.sharpness_max = sharp;
 		f.focus_max_pos = focus_pos;
 	}
 
 	focus_pos += f.step;
-	if (focus_pos <= f.stop)
+	if (focus_pos <= f.stop) {
+		f.skip = kSettleStats;
 		return;
+	}
 
 	if (f.phase == 0) {
 		f.phase = 1;
 		f.start = std::clamp(f.focus_max_pos - kFineSpan, 0.0, 100.0);
 		f.stop = std::clamp(f.focus_max_pos + kFineSpan, 0.0, 100.0);
 		f.step = kFineStep;
-		f.skip = 1;
+		f.skip = kSettleStats + 1;
 		f.sharpness_max = 0;
 		focus_pos = f.start;
 		return;
@@ -276,7 +288,8 @@
 	/* Done: keep sharpness_max to detect a scene change */
 	sweep = false;
 	f.lost = 0;
-	f.skip = 1;
+	f.skip = kSettleStats + 1;
+	rebase_ = kRebaseStats;
 	focus_pos = f.focus_max_pos;
 	k.afState = f.sharpness_max > kFlatSharpness ? controls::AfStateFocused
 						     : controls::AfStateFailed;
